WHAT YOU ALREADY KNOW   What you already know
Chapter 12 set out the shape of a cash budget and said that a forecast shows when a shortfall arrives rather than whether. This chapter fills the budget in with numbers, and first explains why any cash is held at all.
13.1  Three motives for holding cash
The classical analysis gives three reasons, and the exam asks for them by name.
The transaction motive. Payments and receipts do not coincide. Payroll falls due on a day, suppliers on another, customers pay when they pay, and a balance is needed so that the ordinary business of the month can be conducted without borrowing on a Tuesday to repay on a Thursday. The size of this requirement depends on the volume of transactions and on how well they can be timed.
The precautionary motive. The unexpected happens: a customer fails, a machine breaks, a shipment is delayed, a tax assessment arrives. A buffer absorbs the shock without forcing a sale of assets or a hurried loan. The size of this requirement depends on how variable the flows are and on how quickly the company could borrow instead — which is why a company with a committed, undrawn line of credit can hold less cash than one without, and the line is a substitute for the buffer.
The speculative motive. Opportunities arrive at short notice: a competitor's assets at a price, a supplier offering a large discount for a bulk purchase, a company for sale. Cash is the ability to act, and a company with none cannot. In practice this is the motive most often given and least often true, since surplus cash is more usually the absence of a decision than the preparation for one.
A fourth reason is institutional rather than classical and appears in questions constantly. A compensating balance is a minimum balance a bank requires a borrower to keep in its account as a condition of a loan or a line of credit. It is not available for use, so it is cash in form and not in substance, and it raises the effective cost of the borrowing — a calculation Chapter 18 performs.
Figure F613-02 sets the four out.
Figure F613-02. Why a company holds cash
@table
Motive | What it funds | What increases it | What substitutes for it
Transaction | The timing gap in ordinary business | Volume, and poor timing of flows | Better scheduling, faster collection
Precautionary | The unexpected | Variability of flows | A committed line of credit
Speculative | Opportunities at short notice | A strategy of acquisition | Rapid access to borrowing
Compensating balance | A bank's requirement | The terms of the facility | Negotiating a fee instead
@end
