13.4  Longer horizons and other methods
The receipts and disbursements method is exact and demanding: it needs a collection pattern, supplier terms and a payment schedule, and beyond a few months the detail is spurious. For a longer horizon the adjusted net income method is used instead, and it is the indirect cash flow statement of Part 1 run forwards.
Start with forecast net income. Add back the non-cash charges — depreciation, amortisation, impairment. Adjust for the expected movements in working capital: an increase in receivables or inventory is a use of cash, an increase in payables a source. Deduct the planned capital expenditure, the debt repayments and the dividends. What is left is the expected change in cash.
The two methods answer different questions and the exam distinguishes them. The receipts method tells a treasurer which week needs a facility. The adjusted net income method tells a board whether next year's plan is fundable at all. A company needs both, at different resolutions: daily for a fortnight, weekly for a quarter, monthly for a year, annually for the plan.
Three techniques improve any of them.
A rolling horizon. Re-cut every month so that the view ahead never shortens.
Scenarios. A base case, a case in which collections run a week slower, and a case in which a large customer fails. The second of these is the assumption that most often breaks, and testing it is cheap.
A reconciliation to the balance sheet. The forecast's closing receivables should equal the sales not yet collected under the assumed pattern, and its closing inventory should equal the cover the purchasing plan implies. A forecast that does not reconcile contains an error that the cash line alone will hide.
Figure F613-04 compares the methods.
Figure F613-04. Two forecasting methods
@table
Feature | Receipts and disbursements | Adjusted net income
Built from | Individual flows and their timing | Forecast profit and balance movements
Horizon | Days to a few months | Quarters to years
Answers | When will we be short, and by how much? | Is the plan fundable?
Needs | Collection pattern, supplier terms | A forecast income statement and balance sheet
Weakness | Spurious precision at long range | No detail about timing within a period
@end
