13.5  Acting on the answer
A forecast produces one of two findings, and each has a short list of responses.
A forecast shortfall. In order of preference: collect faster, which Chapter 14 is about; slow payments within the terms agreed, which Chapter 14 also covers; draw on a committed line of credit, which is why it was arranged; delay discretionary spending, such as a capital project or a dividend; sell marketable securities, which Chapter 15 arranged to mature at the right moment; and only then negotiate new borrowing, which is the most expensive because it is the least prepared.
A forecast surplus. In order of preference: fund the investment opportunities, which Chapter 8 showed outrank everything; repay expensive short-term debt; place the money in marketable securities matched to the date it will be needed, which Chapter 15 selects; and, where the surplus is permanent rather than temporary, return it to shareholders by the dividend or buyback of Chapters 10 and 11.
Orontes's $7,302,100 is a surplus by the measure of section 13.2 — 75.9 days of operating payments against a 39.8-day cash cycle and a bank relationship that works. The order above gives the answer Book 2 has been building towards: fund the two projects at 14 and 11 per cent, which absorb $6,000,000; place the rest for the dates the forecast says it will be needed; and hold the dividend where it is until the gross margin has stopped falling. What should not happen is what happened in 2025, which is that the balance grew by $5,202,100 and sat in a current account earning nothing while the company's cost of capital ran at 9.04 per cent.
