LANGUAGE FOCUS   "Liquid"
English liquid in finance means convertible into cash quickly and without loss of value, and liquidity is that quality. The metaphor is of something that flows, and it is used in three related senses that a candidate must keep apart: an asset is liquid if it can be sold quickly; a company is liquid if it can meet its obligations; and a market is liquid if a large order can be executed without moving the price. Arabic سيولة covers all three with the same metaphor. French liquidité does the same, and French adds disponibilités for the cash itself — the money immediately available — which is a distinction English makes only with the clumsy phrase cash and cash equivalents. Note that illiquid never means insolvent: a company can be solvent and illiquid, which is exactly the case this chapter exists to prevent.
