Case-style practice: Orontes's cash
Orontes's treasurer presents the 2026 cash plan. The company holds $7,302,100, equal to 75.9 days of operating payments. Its cash cycle is 39.8 days, it has a working relationship with Levant Commerce Bank at 6 per cent, short-dated government securities yield 4 per cent, and its weighted average cost of capital is 9.04 per cent. Two projects returning 14 and 11 per cent need $6,000,000 during the year, with payments spread over four quarters. The board's current policy is a minimum balance of $2,000,000.
Required. Say whether the balance is appropriate, what you would do with it through the year, and what you would change about the policy. Under 300 words.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
Answers and explanations
Section checks
SC13-1  Answer A. Ordinary business has a timing gap.
B is wrong. That is the speculative motive.
C is wrong. That is a compensating balance.
D is wrong. Cash earns little or nothing, which is the cost of holding it.
SC13-2  Answer A. A facility stands in for a buffer against the unexpected.
B is wrong. Transaction balances fund day-to-day timing.
C is wrong. A facility rarely funds an opportunistic purchase quickly enough.
D is wrong. A compensating balance is a requirement, not a motive.
SC13-3  Answer A. It is required, unusable, and raises the effective rate.
B is wrong. That is the speculative motive.
C is wrong. A precautionary balance is the company's own choice.
D is wrong. Its unavailability is the point.
SC13-4  Answer A. Predictability reduces the buffer needed.
B is wrong. More cash is needed when flows are uncertain.
C is wrong. The requirement differs.
D is wrong. Transaction balances are still needed.
SC13-5  Answer A. 7,302,100 ÷ 96,164 = 75.9 days.
B is wrong. This misplaces the decimal.
C is wrong. This misplaces it the other way.
D is wrong. Both figures are given.
SC13-6  Answer A. The forgone return is the cost of holding cash.
B is wrong. A higher rate raises the opportunity cost.
C is wrong. The cost moves with the rate.
D is wrong. Nothing compels a company to invest its balances.
SC13-7  Answer A. 0.20 × 1,400,000 = 280,000.
B is wrong. That is the whole month's sales.
C is wrong. That is the 80 per cent collected later.
D is wrong. That is half the sales.
SC13-8  Answer A. The policy minimum is the floor.
B is wrong. Running to zero leaves no margin.
C is wrong. The opening balance is an input, not the test.
D is wrong. Profit is not a cash measure.
SC13-9  Answer A. Growth pays out before it collects.
B is wrong. Nothing here suggests misstatement.
C is wrong. Depreciation is not a cash flow.
D is wrong. The sales are real and uncollected.
SC13-10  Answer A. It runs the indirect cash flow statement forwards.
B is wrong. The collection pattern belongs to the receipts method.
C is wrong. The bank statement is history.
D is wrong. The dividend is one deduction among several.
SC13-11  Answer A. Money goes into stock and does not come back this period.
B is wrong. An increase in an asset uses cash.
C is wrong. Working-capital movements are central to the method.
D is wrong. Buying inventory is not an expense until it is sold.
SC13-12  Answer A. The cheapest responses come first.
B is wrong. Borrowing at short notice is the most expensive route.
C is wrong. A dividend cut is a large signal for a timing problem.
D is wrong. Selling a fixed asset to fund a month is disproportionate.
SC13-13  Answer A. Projects above the cost of capital create value.
B is wrong. A buyback returns money that could have earned more.
C is wrong. The same objection applies.
D is wrong. Placing the money is what to do with what remains.
SC13-14  Answer A. The money earns nothing against a cost of 9.04 per cent.
B is wrong. Prudence has a price and it can be excessive.
C is wrong. Idle cash enlarges the denominator and lowers the return.
D is wrong. No standard sets a cash balance.
Your turn
@table
Item | Answer
Collections from June sales | 225,000: 0.25 × 900,000 = 225,000.
Collections from May sales | 440,000: 0.55 × 800,000 = 440,000.
Collections from April sales | 126,000: 0.18 × 700,000 = 126,000.
Total receipts | 791,000: 225,000 + 440,000 + 126,000 = 791,000.
Payments | 850,000: 600,000 + 180,000 + 70,000 = 850,000.
Closing cash | 61,000: 120,000 + 791,000 − 850,000 = 61,000.
Shortfall against a 200,000 minimum | 139,000: 200,000 − 61,000 = 139,000.
What it tells you | The month is close to break-even in cash and short against policy, and the company needs 139,000 of facility or acceleration. Note that 2 per cent of each month's sales is never collected on these percentages, which is a credit-policy question for Chapter 16 rather than a cash one.
@end
