Case-style set: Orontes's cash
Is the balance appropriate? No. At 75.9 days of operating payments against a 39.8-day cash cycle, a working bank relationship and no large debt maturity, the company is holding roughly twice what its operations require. The cost is visible: at the 4 per cent available on short-dated government securities, the idle portion forgoes real money, and measured against the 9.04 per cent cost of capital the whole balance is expensive to carry.
What I would do through the year. Fund the two projects first: $6,000,000 at 14 and 11 per cent, well above the cost of capital, and payable over four quarters rather than at once. Build a quarterly profile of those payments from the project plans, then place the cash in securities maturing on those dates — a laddered portfolio of three, six, nine and twelve-month instruments, which Chapter 15 selects. That converts an idle balance into a scheduled one and earns the 4 per cent on everything not yet needed, without any risk of having to sell early.
Keep the remainder as the working balance, sized at around thirty days of operating payments, which is roughly $2,900,000 and comfortably covers the 39.8-day cycle given a committed facility.
What I would change about the policy. The minimum balance of $2,000,000 is expressed as an amount and should be expressed in days of operating payments, so that it scales with the business instead of being revisited by memory. I would also ask the board to agree a standing instruction that any balance above the policy minimum is placed in marketable securities matched to forecast needs, so that the decision not to invest has to be taken deliberately rather than by default — which is how five million dollars came to be sitting in a current account in the first place.
